nty"], "rationale": "Gillen pairs a roughly 17-to-1 cash advantage with uniformly favorable expert ratings and market pricing near 90 percent, but the two-point district, the absence of any public polls, and a Tilt D rating from Inside Elections argue against calling this Safe.", "watch": "Any public poll showing Driscoll within single digits, or a late wave of Republican outside spending, would signal a genuinely competitive race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Laura Gillen (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Democrat Laura Gillen defends a Long Island district that leans Democratic by two points, facing Republican Jeanine Driscoll, who won her party's primary in June. No public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals, expert ratings, and prediction markets.\n\nGillen holds an enormous financial edge, about $3.8 million on hand against roughly $226,000 for Driscoll, and has stayed visible with police ride-alongs and federal water-infrastructure funding for the area. Every major ratings outlet favors her, spanning Tilt D to Safe D, and prediction markets put her odds near 90 percent. That adds up to a clear but not commanding position: the narrowly partisan seat, the lack of polling, and a contested campaign over taxes and local issues keep this short of Safe, while the money gap and incumbency make an upset unlikely.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9622, "margin": 11.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9401, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9422, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9272, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9344, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9323, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9557, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9565, "margin": 11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9413, "ma